<%# A tap card's response strip — the 2-6 answers a respondent chooses between,
    over the bottom scrim of the swipe card.

    Locals:
      responses — a resolved TapScales.for_card array (ordered most-negative
        first; each entry carries its key, label, mark, colour and direction)
      editable  — editor mode: labels become contenteditable and each response
        gains the 🎨 style popover and a delete button

    The player renders each response as a real <button> with the label inside,
    so the whole circle-and-caption (or the whole pill) is one target with one
    accessible name. The editor can't: the label has to hold a caret, and a
    contenteditable inside a <button> doesn't reliably take one. So it renders
    the same spans in a <div> that carries the same click action — the identical
    split _card_component already makes between `pick_actions` and the editable
    option row, for the identical reason.

    Up to four responses are a row of labelled circles in the bottom scrim. At
    five and up the strip FANS: the pills are placed on an arc over the photo,
    most-negative at the bottom left, the middle at the apex, most-positive at
    the bottom right (TapScales.fan_position). The DOM is the same either way —
    each response just carries its place on the arc as --tap-x / --tap-y, which
    CSS uses only in fan mode — so a creator adding a fifth response doesn't
    rebuild anything.
%>
<% count = responses.length %>
<% fan   = TapScales.fan?(count) %>
<div class="rotate-actions<%= " rotate-actions--fan" if fan %>"
     data-tap-responses
     data-response-count="<%= count %>">
  <% responses.each_with_index do |response, i| %>
    <%
      key   = response["key"]
      label = response["label"].to_s
      # The response's hue rides in as a custom property so one rule paints the
      # ring, the pill border and the drag-intent highlight from a single value.
      # Its place on the arc rides in the same way, and is simply ignored by the
      # row layout — one set of attributes, whichever shape the strip is in.
      style = +""
      style << "--tap-response-color: #{response['color']};" if response["color"].present?
      style << "--tap-x: #{response['x']}%; --tap-y: #{response['y']}%;" if fan
      classes = [ "rotate-action" ]
      classes << "is-strong" if response["strong"]
    %>
    <% if editable %>
      <div class="<%= classes.join(" ") %>"
           style="<%= style %>"
           data-tap-response
           data-response-key="<%= key %>"
           data-response-glyph="<%= response["glyph"] %>"
           data-action="click->tap-stack#pick"
           data-tap-stack-key="<%= key %>"
           data-tap-stack-direction="<%= response["direction"] %>"
           <%= option_style_data_attrs(response) %>>
        <%# The mark itself opens the style popover: "users want to be able to
            update the icons", and the icon is what they click — the hover-only
            17px 🎨 beside it was never found. The span stays aria-hidden
            (decorative to AT); keyboard and AT users have the focusable 🎨
            button below, which reveals itself on focus-within. option-style#open
            stops propagation, so this no longer falls through to the row's
            tap-stack#pick preview. %>
        <span class="rotate-action-btn rotate-action-btn--editable" aria-hidden="true"
              data-action="click->option-style#open"
              title="<%= t("card.style_option") %>"><%= tap_response_mark(response, index: i) %></span>
        <span class="rotate-action-label" contenteditable="true"
              data-tap-response-label><%= label %></span>
        <button type="button" class="option-style-btn tap-response-style-btn"
                data-action="click->option-style#open"
                title="<%= t("card.style_option") %>">🎨</button>
        <button type="button" class="tap-response-delete"
                data-action="click->card-editor#deleteResponse"
                title="<%= t("card.remove_response", default: "Remove this answer") %>"
                aria-label="<%= t("card.remove_response", default: "Remove this answer") %>">×</button>
      </div>
    <% else %>
      <button type="button" class="<%= classes.join(" ") %>"
              style="<%= style %>"
              data-tap-response
              data-response-key="<%= key %>"
              data-action="click->tap-stack#pick"
              data-tap-stack-key="<%= key %>"
              data-tap-stack-direction="<%= response["direction"] %>"
              aria-label="<%= label %>">
        <span class="rotate-action-btn" aria-hidden="true"><%= tap_response_mark(response, index: i) %></span>
        <span class="rotate-action-label"><%= label %></span>
      </button>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <%# The ＋ that used to sit here is gone: two controls set the same number
      and the card carried the weaker one. "Answers per statement" in the
      settings panel says how many there are and shows the range at a glance,
      where the ＋ only ever offered "one more" and had to hide itself at the
      ceiling to avoid offering nothing. The per-answer × below stays — removing
      a specific answer is not the same question as how many there should be.
      Growing the scale from the panel now KEEPS the creator's wording
      (card-editor#_keepCreatorContent); before, it reseeded every label from
      the preset, which is why the ＋ existed. %>
</div>
